\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Cycle decimation and periodic covariance}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take the unit-weight cycle on #{\mathbb Z/(2N)}, with
#{N\ge3} and precision #{Q=L_{C_{2N}}+aI}, where
#{a=m^2>0}. The group #{G=C_N} acts by two-site rotations.
It preserves the even retained vertices and the odd eliminated
vertices. Write #{x_j=\phi_{2j}}, #{y_j=\phi_{2j+1}} and
#{q=2+a}; indices #{j} lie in #{\mathbb Z/N}.
For the shift #{(Tz)_j=z_{j-1}}, the blocks are
##{A=C=qI,\qquad B=-(I+T),\qquad B^*=-(I+T^{-1}).}
Thus the [equivariant Schur formula](fdip-0056) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=qI-q^{-1}(2I+T+T^{-1})\\
 &=w'L_{C_N}+a'I,\\
w'&=\frac1{2+a},\qquad
 a'=(2+a)-\frac4{2+a}
    =\frac{a(a+4)}{a+2}>0.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{L_{C_N}=2I-T-T^{-1}}. The restriction #{Q_{RR}=qI}
has no off-diagonal entries, while #{S} has entries #{-1/q}
between adjacent retained vertices. The retained set is stable under
#{G}, but the marginal precision is induced by elimination.}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  retained/.style={circle,draw,fill=black!18,minimum size=6mm,inner sep=1pt},
  removed/.style={circle,draw,dashed,minimum size=6mm,inner sep=1pt},
  every node/.style={font=\small}
]
\node[retained] (v0) at (0,1.25) {$0$};
\node[removed] (v1) at (1.08,0.625) {$1$};
\node[retained] (v2) at (1.08,-0.625) {$2$};
\node[removed] (v3) at (0,-1.25) {$3$};
\node[retained] (v4) at (-1.08,-0.625) {$4$};
\node[removed] (v5) at (-1.08,0.625) {$5$};
\draw (v0)--(v1)--(v2)--(v3)--(v4)--(v5)--(v0);
\draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (1.6,0)--node[above,align=center]
 {eliminate\\odd vertices} (3.4,0);
\node[retained] (r0) at (4.9,1.25) {$0$};
\node[retained] (r2) at (5.98,-0.625) {$2$};
\node[retained] (r4) at (3.82,-0.625) {$4$};
\draw (r0)--(r2)--(r4)--(r0);
\node at (0,-1.9) {$w=1,\quad m^2=a$};
\node at (4.9,-1.9) {$w'=1/q,\quad m'^2=q-4/q$};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The six-cycle illustrates #{N=3}. Shaded even vertices remain;
dashed odd vertices are integrated out. Each triangle edge has
weight #{1/q}, and the mass squared changes from #{a} to
#{q-4/q}, with #{q=2+a}.}

\p{The finite Gaussian normalization is retained exactly:
#{Z_Q=Z_CZ_S} with #{Z_C=(2\pi/q)^{N/2}}, and the retained
law is #{\mu_S}. The effective weight and mass are both part of
this law. Dividing #{S} by #{w'} is useful for comparing its
parameters, but changes the field covariance by a scalar unless
the corresponding field rescaling is also made.}

\p{Put #{q=2\cosh\omega} with #{\omega>0}. Then
##{2+\frac{a'}{w'}=q^2-2=2\cosh(2\omega).}
The doubled parameter has an exact finite-ring interpretation.
For #{M\ge3}, the covariance of precision
#{w(2\cosh\eta\,I-T-T^{-1})} on #{\mathbb Z/M}, with
#{w,\eta>0}, is the periodic kernel
##{\begin{aligned}
K_{M,w,\eta}(r)
 &=\frac{e^{-\eta r}+e^{-\eta(M-r)}}
 {2w\sinh\eta\,(1-e^{-M\eta})},\\
&\hspace{24mm}0\le r\le M-1.
\end{aligned}}
For the original and retained fields this gives
##{(Q^{-1})_{0,2r}
 =K_{2N,1,\omega}(2r)
 =K_{N,w',2\omega}(r)
 =(S^{-1})_{0,r}.}
The equality is between covariances with their actual weights.}

\proof{
\p{On the integer line, put
#{h(n)=e^{-\eta|n|}/(2w\sinh\eta)}.
For #{n\ne0}, the two geometric progressions give
#{w(2\cosh\eta\,h(n)-h(n-1)-h(n+1))=0}.
At #{n=0} the expression equals
#{(2\cosh\eta-2e^{-\eta})/(2\sinh\eta)=1}.
Periodize this identity by summing #{h(r+kM)} over
#{k\in\mathbb Z}; absolute convergence permits the finite
difference operation under the sum. The result is the unit point
source on #{\mathbb Z/M}, so the periodized kernel is the inverse
of the positive precision. Summing the geometric series gives
#{K_{M,w,\eta}}.}

\p{For #{M=2N} and even separation #{2r}, the numerator and
periodic denominator equal those for #{M=N}, #{\eta=2\omega}.
Their remaining constants agree because
##{2w'\sinh(2\omega)
 =\frac{2\sinh(2\omega)}{2\cosh\omega}
 =2\sinh\omega.}
This proves the covariance identity directly, in agreement with
Gaussian marginalization.}
}

\p{In the convention that correlation length is the reciprocal decay
rate of these periodized exponential images, it changes from
#{\xi=1/\omega} fine lattice units to
#{\xi'=1/(2\omega)=\xi / 2} coarse lattice units. The finite ring
has two winding directions, so its covariance is not a single
exponential in separation. If the old spacing is #{\ell}, the new
spacing is #{2\ell}; the physical decay length
#{\ell / \omega=(2\ell)/(2\omega)} is unchanged.}

\p{The bound #{N\ge3} keeps the reduced graph a simple cycle.
At #{N=2}, #{T=T^{-1}} and two eliminated vertices connect the
same retained pair, producing two parallel edges of weight #{1/q},
or one edge of weight #{2/q}. At #{a=0}, the algebraic formula
still gives #{w'=1/2} and #{a'=0}, the two-resistors-in-series
limit. The full precision then has a constant zero mode and no
normalized full-space Gaussian law. Pinning or a mean-zero
constraint must be specified as in
[constrained massless laws](fdip-003V); the positive-mass covariance
formula is not a finite limit on the full field space.}

\p{This is a direct cycle calculation within
\newvocab{Kron reduction}, the Schur reduction of graph Laplacians
with possible positive diagonal terms.
\citet{Lemma 2.1, pp. 5-6, and Lemma 3.3, p. 14, arXiv version}{dorfler2011kron}
proves closure and the equivalence of successive and simultaneous
eliminations. In that source the diagonal mass corresponds to a
shunt conductance or graph self-loop. In the incidence convention
used here an edge with identical endpoints has zero difference;
the mass is therefore kept as the separate term #{aI}.
The source supplies the general reduction context, while the
periodic covariance and scale statements are proved above.}
